  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/37ec9bf31a46129d10531f179f16f1f8bb42b88428652ec02e1022ba958b51d5.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $h$ is part of the graph of the function $y=5-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}).$ Find the exact value of $y$ when $x=10.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{10\\pi }{3}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10\\pi }{3}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{10\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $y=5-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=5+\\sqrt{3}.$",
